index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..a590f0e99acce66c0df319b9422f731439f096e5
--- /dev/null
+++ b/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,130 @@
+# Verified problem generator for arXiv:1707.08730
+
+| profile field | value |
+|---|---|
+| Track | **B — no-tool compression** |
+| Native domain | combinatorics |
+| Object regime | finite discrete |
+| Computational core | subset sum |
+| Certificate | integer tuple (six increasing indices) |
+| Intended intuition | invariant: a hidden common residue class |
+| Domain essentiality | native; no reduction |
+
+## What the family asks
+
+This module turns Ammar Daskin's [*A Quantum Approach to Subset-Sum and Similar
+Problems*](https://arxiv.org/abs/1707.08730) into exact fixed-cardinality subset-sum
+instances. The solver receives a list of positive integers and a target and must give
+six distinct 0-based indices whose values add to the target. The checker only validates
+the shape of those indices and performs one exact integer sum, and it accepts every
+valid subset rather than comparing with the planted answer.
+
+The construction is inverse generation. It samples equally distributed six-item
+buckets, chooses one bucket uniformly, shuffles every item together, and sets the target
+to the chosen bucket's sum. Plant and decoy buckets therefore come from the same
+distribution. Section I of the paper supplies the exact subset-sum definition; Section
+III identifies a subset by the computational-basis index paired with its sum.
+
+## Why this is Track B
+
+This is deliberately **not** a claim of computational hardness. Section I names
+exhaustive search and `O(nW)` dynamic programming and quotes a quantum-walk cost
+polynomial in `n` when subset size is fixed. For the fixed size six used here, the
+standard classical split into two triples is an exact `Theta(n^3)` meet-in-the-middle
+algorithm. At shipping `n=150`, the implementation solved 8/8 instances with a median
+2,040,862 primitive operations and 0.224 seconds.
+
+The compression is an invariant: all six selected weights have one residue modulo 997.
+After noticing it, the target identifies that residue and a single scan identifies the
+six indices. This takes 158 exact arithmetic operations at shipping size, within the
+no-tool cap, but entails 150 exact reductions of 59-bit integers. Sections III–IV's
+quantum procedure is conditional on Assumptions 1 and 2; it is not used to generate or
+grade these instances.
+
+## Worked demo
+
+For `make_instance(n=12, k=6, modulus=997, quotient_bits=5, seed=4)`, the complete
+weight list is:
+
+```text
+0:23241  1:31148  2:24238  3:19253  4:20181  5:17259
+6:18187  7:21247  8:27229  9:16193  10:25166  11:28157
+target = 139032
+```
+
+The answer is `[1, 4, 6, 9, 10, 11]`.
+`verify(inst, [1, 4, 6, 9, 10, 11])` returns `(True, "ok")`, while dropping the final
+index returns `(False, "answer has fewer than 6 indices")`. A person can solve this
+demo on paper by inspecting the two residue buckets and checking six additions.
+
+## Difficulty presets
+
+| preset | items `n` | chosen `k` | quotient bits | status |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| demo | 12 | 6 | 5 | hand-solvable illustration |
+| easy | 150 | 6 | 56 | intended shipping preset |
+| medium | 210 | 6 | 72 | harder, fixed-length answer |
+| hard | 270 | 6 | 88 | harder, fixed-length answer |
+
+`escalate()` keeps the witness at six indices: it first grows the item set and then
+raises coefficient entropy. It never lengthens the answer.
+
+## Gate results
+
+| gate | measured result |
+|---|---|
+| G1 | 12/12 planted witnesses verify; 12/12 JSON round trips |
+| G2 | five corruptions rejected with five distinct reasons |
+| G3 | realistic tagged response round-trips; garbage returns `None` |
+| G4 | 0/200,000 structure-aware guesses; `C(150,6)=14,297,000,725` candidates |
+| G5 | shipping sampled density 0/200,000; demo has exactly 1/924 answers; reference cost 2,017,318 operations and 0.234 s on the recorded seed |
+| G6 | four attacks each 0/8; exact meet-in-the-middle and compact reference each 8/8 |
+| G7 | doubled `n=300` builds and verifies; search space 962,822,846,700 |
+| G8 | 80/80 symmetry checks and 80/80 carried witnesses; 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
+| G9(c) | 23 answer characters, 6 atoms, about 6 tokens; intended route 158 operations |
+| G9(a,b) | pending: OpenRouter returned HTTP 403 `Key limit exceeded` before a valid oracle attempt |
+
+## Oracle loop and G9 arms
+
+The mandatory harness was invoked, but every redraw was rejected by OpenRouter before
+model execution. These errors are infrastructure failures and are not counted as model
+failures. Consequently no hardness verdict or G9 claim is currently made.
+
+| run | preset | valid attempts | solved | result |
+|---|---|---:|---:|---|
+| bare | easy | 0 | 0 | blocked by OpenRouter key limit |
+| hinted | easy | 0 | 0 | not run while the same limit persists |
+| placebo | easy | 0 | 0 | not run while the same limit persists |
+
+The hinted-minus-placebo diagnostic is therefore undefined, not zero. Once access is
+restored, run each arm in a separate directory as required; only a `hardened` hinted
+verdict can pass G9(b).
+
+## Use
+
+```python
+from gen_1707_08730 import DIFFICULTY, make_instance, render, verify
+
+inst = make_instance(seed=123, **DIFFICULTY["easy"])
+print(render(inst))
+assert verify(inst, inst["answer"]) == (True, "ok")
+```
+
+After the oracle evidence and all gates pass, emit fresh records from this directory
+with:
+
+```bash
+../../scripts/emit.sh 1707.08730 20 easy
+```
+
+## Caveats
+
+Knowledge of modulus 997 makes the family linear-time, and the generic exact reference
+algorithm already solves shipping instances in well under a second; this is only a
+no-tool benchmark. The 0/200,000 guess estimate is for a uniform prior over increasing
+six-subsets, not for a solver exploiting generation structure. The adversary panel
+tests magnitude, descending greedy choice, random restarts, and an obvious decimal-
+suffix ansatz, but does not test LLL, ILP, or every possible modular-statistics attack.
+Accidental alternative six-subsets are allowed and would be accepted. Standard-library
+integer arithmetic suffices, so `gvlib` is intentionally not imported. Most importantly,
+the oracle and G9 gates remain unverified until the external key limit is cleared.
