codex
Implemented a self-contained Track B subset-sum generator based on the paper’s native problem definition and basis-index witness. The paper explicitly discusses exhaustive search, pseudo-polynomial dynamic programming, and fixed-subset-size algorithms, so a Track A claim would be dishonest. [Paper PDF](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1707.08730)

Files produced:

- [gen_1707_08730.py](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1707.08730/gen_1707_08730.py)
- [selftest_report.json](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1707.08730/selftest_report.json)
- [README.md](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1707.08730/README.md)
- [llm_loop_transcript.jsonl](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1707.08730/llm_loop_transcript.jsonl)

G1–G8 pass. Highlights:

- 0/200,000 structure-aware random guesses
- 14,297,000,725 candidate six-subsets
- Four adversarial attacks: 0/8 each
- Exact meet-in-the-middle reference: 8/8, median 2,040,862 operations
- Compact residue route: 158 operations
- Answer: six indices, 23 measured characters
- Canonical-key checks: 80/80 invariant, 20/20 unrelated instances distinct

The only blocker is external: both hardening attempts received HTTP 403 `Key limit exceeded` from OpenRouter on every redraw. Therefore G9 remains explicitly failing/pending, and the G9 transcript files were not fabricated. A replenished `OPENROUTER_API_KEY` is required to finish the bare, hinted, and placebo oracle runs.
diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
