Step 0 supports only a Track B interpretation for this construction. The paper’s exact witness is a subset, cheaply checked by integer addition, but fixed-cardinality subset sum has a classical meet-in-the-middle algorithm; the paper itself also cites polynomial-in-\(n\) quantum-walk cost when the subset size is fixed. The paper’s own proposed polynomial quantum routine is conditional on Assumptions 1 and 2, so it does not furnish a dependable generator certificate. I’m retaining inverse generation and will disclose the exact classical solver and measured cost.
