+instances. The solver receives positive integers and a target, then returns six
+distinct 0-based indices whose weights add to that target. Verification checks the
+index syntax and performs one exact integer sum; it accepts any valid six-subset, not
+just the planted one.
 
-The construction is inverse generation. It samples equally distributed six-item
-buckets, chooses one bucket uniformly, shuffles every item together, and sets the target
-to the chosen bucket's sum. Plant and decoy buckets therefore come from the same
-distribution. Section I of the paper supplies the exact subset-sum definition; Section
-III identifies a subset by the computational-basis index paired with its sum.
+Generation is inverse, never a search. The generator makes equally sized residue
+buckets, chooses one bucket uniformly, shuffles all weights together, and defines the
+target as the chosen bucket's sum. Planted and decoy buckets use the same sampling
+rule. Section I of the paper fixes the exact subset-sum definition, and Section III
+represents a subset by a computational-basis index paired with its sum. The modular
+invariant is generator-added structure, but the objects and verifier remain the
+paper's native integers, target, and subset.
 
 ## Why this is Track B
 
-This is deliberately **not** a claim of computational hardness. Section I names
-exhaustive search and `O(nW)` dynamic programming and quotes a quantum-walk cost
-polynomial in `n` when subset size is fixed. For the fixed size six used here, the
-standard classical split into two triples is an exact `Theta(n^3)` meet-in-the-middle
-algorithm. At shipping `n=150`, the implementation solved 8/8 instances with a median
-2,040,862 primitive operations and 0.242 seconds on the recorded gate run.
+This is not a structural-hardness claim. Section I describes exhaustive search and
+`O(nW)` dynamic programming and cites a quantum-walk bound polynomial in `n` when the
+subset size is fixed. For the fixed size six here, the disclosed exact classical
+reference algorithm splits a candidate into two triples and uses meet-in-the-middle
+in `Theta(n^3)` time and memory. On the final shipping gate at `n=150`, it solved 8/8
+instances with a median 2,040,862 counted integer/hash operations and 2.448 seconds.
 
-The compression is an invariant: all six selected weights have one residue modulo 997.
-After noticing it, the target identifies that residue and a single scan identifies the
-six indices. This takes 158 exact arithmetic operations at shipping size, within the
-no-tool cap, but entails 150 exact reductions of 66-bit integers. Sections III–IV's
-quantum procedure is conditional on Assumptions 1 and 2; it is not used to generate or
-grade these instances.
+The compressed route is to notice that all six desired weights have the same residue
+modulo 997. The target then determines that residue and one scan recovers the six
+indices. The measured route is 158 exact operations. The gap is therefore about 2.04
+million operations versus 158, but a bare no-tool solver must first discover the
+unannounced modulus. Sections III–IV's proposed quantum method is conditional on
+Assumptions 1 and 2; it is not used to manufacture or certify these instances.
 
